# BRIDGEFY — OFFLINE DISASTER COORDINATION NETWORK

## Final Project Document

> **When everything fails, Bridgefy doesn't.**

> [!CAUTION]
> **Name Conflict:** "Bridgefy" is an existing mesh-messaging company ([bridgefy.me](https://bridgefy.me)). **Strongly consider renaming** before presenting to judges — alternatives: **ResQLink**, **GridPulse**, **SafeSignal**, **MeshAlert**.

---

## 1. Elevator Pitch

Bridgefy is an Android app that turns every phone into a node in an invisible emergency network. When cell towers and internet are destroyed, victims send distress signals, rescue teams coordinate response, and officials broadcast verified alerts — **all without internet**. Messages hop silently phone-to-phone through human movement. A Gateway at the disaster's edge bridges the mesh to a cloud dashboard accessible worldwide. People without the app connect through a Wi-Fi portal — **no download needed**.

---

## 2. Architecture — Three Zones

```
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  ZONE A — DISASTER ZONE  (No Internet)                           │
│                                                                  │
│  📱 Citizen ◄──BLE/WiFi──► 📱 Citizen ◄──BLE/WiFi──► 📱 Field  │
│       │                         │                   Responder    │
│       │                         │                       │        │
│       └─────── BLE/WiFi ────────┘                  BLE/WiFi      │
│                                                         │        │
│                  📱 Legacy Phone ──WiFi──► 📶 Captive Portal     │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                                                         │
                                              Satellite / LoRa /
                                              4G / SMS
                                                         │
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  ZONE C — THE BRIDGE  (Edge of Disaster)                         │
│  📡 Gateway Node (phone with internet connection)                │
│  Uploads mesh data → Cloud  |  Downloads bulletins → Mesh        │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                                                         │
                                                     Internet
                                                         │
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  ZONE B — COMMAND CENTER  (Remote, Has Internet)                 │
│  🖥️ Web Dashboard: Live SOS map, verified bulletins, ACKs       │
│  🗄️ Firebase Firestore: Persistent data + real-time sync        │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

---

## 3. Five Node Types

| Node | Activation | Capabilities |
|---|---|---|
| **Citizen** | Default on install | 4-button SOS, Volume SOS, Dead Man's Switch, receive bulletins, receive ACKs |
| **Field Responder** | PIN-gated (production: Ed25519 keypair) | All Citizen abilities **+** offline map with SOS pins, mark "En Route" / "Resolved", activate Captive Portal |
| **Gateway** | Auto-activates when internet detected | Mesh participant **+** batches all data → Firebase, injects bulletins back into mesh |
| **Legacy Portal User** | No app needed | Connects to hotspot `EMERGENCY_CONNECT_HERE`, uses browser-based web portal to read bulletins + submit SOS |
| **Command Center** | Web dashboard (separate) | Live map with color-coded SOSes, issue signed bulletins, send signed ACKs to victims |

---

## 4. Seven Core Features

### 🔴 Feature 1: Four-Button SOS Interface

| Button | Status Code | Color |
|---|---|---|
| MEDICAL | `0x01` | Red |
| TRAPPED | `0x02` | Orange |
| HAZARD | `0x03` | Yellow |
| SAFE | `0x04` | Green |

- One tap sends SOS with **automatic GPS** — no typing under stress
- Optional 100-char text note for details
- Rate-limited: **1 SOS per 2 minutes** per device (hardware fingerprinted)

---

### 📳 Feature 2: Volume SOS

- Press **Volume Down 3× fast** = instant distress signal
- Works with **screen off**, in the dark, in your pocket
- **Haptic pulse** confirmation only (silent — critical for stealth in dangerous situations)
- Sends with last known GPS + `TRAPPED` status by default

---

### ⏰ Feature 3: Dead Man's Switch

- If the user doesn't interact with their phone for **45 minutes**, the app auto-broadcasts a **welfare-check beacon**
- Catches **unconscious or incapacitated** victims
- Appears as **amber alert** on responder maps (distinct from active SOS)
- Runs via Android `ForegroundService` with persistent notification

> [!WARNING]
> **Android gotcha:** OEMs like Xiaomi, Samsung, and Huawei aggressively kill background/foreground services. Test on actual demo phones. May need battery optimization exemption.

---

### 📡 Feature 4: Offline Mesh Network

```
Phone A                              Phone B
   │                                    │
   ├── BLE Advertise (custom UUID) ────►│
   │◄── BLE Discover ──────────────────┤
   │                                    │
   ├── Upgrade to Wi-Fi Direct ────────►│
   │                                    │
   ├── Bloom Filter exchange (~1KB) ───►│  (what I've seen)
   │◄── Bloom Filter exchange ─────────┤  (what you've seen)
   │                                    │
   │   Delta transfer: CRITICAL first,  │
   │   then HIGH → NORMAL → LOW         │
   │                                    │
   ├── Verify HMAC integrity ──────────►│
   │◄── Verify HMAC integrity ─────────┤
   │                                    │
   └── Disconnect. Total: <3 seconds ──►│
```

- **BLE** for discovery (low power)
- **Wi-Fi Direct** for high-speed data transfer
- Messages hop automatically — no user action needed
- Max **10 hops** per message (prevents flooding)
- **TTL: 24 hours** default (stale messages auto-purged)

> [!TIP]
> **Hackathon shortcut:** If Bloom filters take too long to implement, fall back to simple **ID-list exchange** (send list of message IDs → partner sends missing ones). Mention Bloom filters as "designed for production."

---

### 🌉 Feature 5: Gateway Bridge

- Any phone with internet connection **auto-activates** as a Gateway
- **Inbound:** Batches all mesh data every 5 minutes → uploads to Firebase Firestore
- **Outbound:** Pulls new bulletins/ACKs from Firebase → injects into mesh
- Multiple gateways can operate simultaneously; Firebase deduplicates via unique message IDs
- If gateway goes down, mesh continues independently; syncs backlog on recovery

---

### 📶 Feature 6: Captive Portal (Legacy Device Support)

- Any Field Responder can activate a Wi-Fi hotspot named `EMERGENCY_CONNECT_HERE`
- Uses **NanoHTTPD** (embedded HTTP server) to serve a simple HTML page
- ₹500 button phones, iPhones, laptops — anything with Wi-Fi + a browser works
- Portal provides: read bulletins + submit SOS (name, location, status)
- **Zero app download required**

> [!NOTE]
> Captive portal auto-redirect behavior varies by device. Have a fallback: instruct users to open browser and navigate to `192.168.49.1`.

---

### 🖥️ Feature 7: Command Center Dashboard

- **Web app** (HTML/JS + Leaflet.js) hosted on Firebase Hosting
- **Live map** with color-coded SOS pins (Red=Medical, Orange=Trapped, Yellow=Hazard, Green=Safe, Amber=Dead Man's Switch)
- **Issue bulletins** — typed into a form, cryptographically signed with Ed25519, pushed to Firebase → Gateway → Mesh
- **Send ACKs** — click an SOS pin → type response (e.g., *"Help dispatched, ETA 30 min"*) → signed and sent back to victim's phone
- **Priority sidebar** — ranks zones by threat density

---

## 5. Message Protocol

### SOS Payload — 26 bytes core

| Field | Size | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `device_id` | 4B | `sha256(android_id + BLE_MAC)` truncated |
| `latitude` | 4B | Float32 |
| `longitude` | 4B | Float32 |
| `status_code` | 1B | `0x01`=Medical, `0x02`=Trapped, `0x03`=Hazard, `0x04`=Safe, `0x05`=DeadManSwitch |
| `lamport_clock` | 4B | Logical timestamp for ordering |
| `ttl_hours` | 1B | Time-to-live (default 24) |
| `hop_count` | 1B | Incremented each relay, max 10 |
| `max_hops` | 1B | Hard limit |
| `hmac` | 4B | Integrity check (truncated HMAC-SHA256) |
| `flags` | 1B | Volume SOS, Dead Man's Switch, etc. |
| `reserved` | 1B | Future use |
| `text_note` | 0–100B | Optional free-text (UTF-8) |

### Bulletin Payload

| Field | Description |
|---|---|
| `alert_type` | Flood / Earthquake / Cyclone / General |
| `affected_zone` | Lat, Lng, Radius (km) |
| `message_text` | Up to 500 chars |
| `ed25519_signature` | 64B — proves it came from Command Center |
| `lamport_clock` | Logical timestamp |

### ACK Payload

| Field | Description |
|---|---|
| `target_device_id` | Which victim this ACK is for |
| `response_text` | *"Help dispatched, ETA 30 min"* |
| `ed25519_signature` | 64B — proves authenticity |

---

## 6. Security Model

| Threat | Mitigation |
|---|---|
| Fake SOS spam | Hardware fingerprint rate limit: **1 SOS per 2 min**. Field Responders can **mute** a device. |
| Fake bulletins / ACKs | **Ed25519 signature** verification. Public key embedded in app. Private key only on Command Center. |
| Message tampering | **HMAC** integrity check per message. Shared key hardcoded in app (hackathon scope). |
| Stale information | **TTL** field — expired messages purged on every sync. |
| Network flooding | **Max hop count** (10). Messages beyond limit dropped. |
| Replay attacks | **Lamport clock** ordering — reject messages with lower clock than last seen from same device. |
| GPS spoofing | Cross-validate GPS against relay chain geography — flag suspicious locations. |

> [!NOTE]
> **Hackathon vs. Production:** The HMAC shared key is hardcoded for the hackathon. In production, this would use per-device keys provisioned during registration, or switch to asymmetric signing.

---

## 7. Power Management

| Mode | Trigger | BLE Scan | GPS | Screen | Battery Life |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Normal** | Default | Every 30s | Every 60s | On | ~8 hrs |
| **Power Saver** | Battery ≤ 30% | Every 5 min | Every 5 min | Dim | ~18 hrs |
| **Emergency Beacon** | Battery ≤ 15% | Minimal broadcast | Off (uses last known) | Off | ~6–8 hrs |

---

## 8. Tech Stack

| Component | Technology | Cost |
|---|---|---|
| Mobile App | Android Native (Kotlin) | Free |
| Mesh Discovery | BLE (Android SDK) | Free |
| Mesh Transfer | Nearby Connections API | Free |
| Local Database | Room (SQLite) | Free |
| Captive Portal | NanoHTTPD | Free |
| Offline Maps | OSMDroid + `.mbtiles` | Free |
| Crypto | TweetNaCl-java (Ed25519) | Free |
| Cloud Backend | Firebase Firestore (free tier) | Free |
| Dashboard | HTML/JS + Leaflet.js | Free |
| Dashboard Hosting | Firebase Hosting (free tier) | Free |
| **Total** | | **₹0** |

> [!WARNING]
> **Firebase free tier limits:** 50K reads/day, 20K writes/day. Fine for demo. Mention *"production would use dedicated cloud"* if judges ask.

